explain.narrative:
## Core concept
A JOIN combines rows from two tables by evaluating a relationship between their columns. A customer row and an order row can be related through customer_id. The join condition determines which row pairs are related; the selected join type determines what happens to unmatched rows.

## SQLite syntax
```sql
SELECT c.customer_name, o.order_id\nFROM customers AS c\nJOIN orders AS o ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id;
```

## Sample schema and data
The examples use a small customers table and an orders table. customer_id is the relationship key. The sample deliberately includes two customers without orders and one order whose customer_id has no matching customer. This lets you observe both matching and unmatched rows.

## Worked examples
### 1. Create two related tables
```sql
CREATE TABLE customers (customer_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT);
CREATE TABLE orders (order_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_id INTEGER, order_total NUMERIC NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO customers VALUES (1,'Asha Rao','Rajahmundry'),(2,'Bilal Khan','Hyderabad'),(3,'Chitra Das','Vijayawada'),(4,'Dev Patel','Mumbai');
INSERT INTO orders VALUES (101,1,120),(102,1,80),(103,3,50),(104,9,40);
SELECT COUNT(*) AS customer_count FROM customers;
```
Expected result: 4

The setup creates four customers and four orders, including one order whose customer identifier has no matching customer row.

### 2. Combine customer and order details
```sql
CREATE TABLE customers (customer_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT);
CREATE TABLE orders (order_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_id INTEGER, order_total NUMERIC NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO customers VALUES (1,'Asha Rao','Rajahmundry'),(2,'Bilal Khan','Hyderabad'),(3,'Chitra Das','Vijayawada'),(4,'Dev Patel','Mumbai');
INSERT INTO orders VALUES (101,1,120),(102,1,80),(103,3,50),(104,9,40);
SELECT c.customer_name, o.order_id, o.order_total FROM customers AS c JOIN orders AS o ON o.customer_id = c.customer_id ORDER BY o.order_id;
```
Expected result: Asha Rao | 101 | 120; Asha Rao | 102 | 80; Chitra Das | 103 | 50

An inner join keeps only row pairs that satisfy the equality condition.

### 3. Join using table aliases
```sql
CREATE TABLE customers (customer_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT);
CREATE TABLE orders (order_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_id INTEGER, order_total NUMERIC NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO customers VALUES (1,'Asha Rao','Rajahmundry'),(2,'Bilal Khan','Hyderabad'),(3,'Chitra Das','Vijayawada'),(4,'Dev Patel','Mumbai');
INSERT INTO orders VALUES (101,1,120),(102,1,80),(103,3,50),(104,9,40);
SELECT c.customer_name, o.order_total FROM customers AS c INNER JOIN orders AS o ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id ORDER BY c.customer_id, o.order_total DESC;
```
Expected result: Asha Rao | 120; Asha Rao | 80; Chitra Das | 50

Aliases shorten qualified column references and make multi-table queries easier to read.

### 4. Join and filter the combined rows
```sql
CREATE TABLE customers (customer_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT);
CREATE TABLE orders (order_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_id INTEGER, order_total NUMERIC NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO customers VALUES (1,'Asha Rao','Rajahmundry'),(2,'Bilal Khan','Hyderabad'),(3,'Chitra Das','Vijayawada'),(4,'Dev Patel','Mumbai');
INSERT INTO orders VALUES (101,1,120),(102,1,80),(103,3,50),(104,9,40);
SELECT c.city, o.order_id FROM customers AS c JOIN orders AS o ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id WHERE o.order_total >= 80 ORDER BY o.order_id;
```
Expected result: Rajahmundry | 101; Rajahmundry | 102

The WHERE condition filters joined rows after matching is logically established.

### 5. Count related records per customer
```sql
CREATE TABLE customers (customer_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT);
CREATE TABLE orders (order_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_id INTEGER, order_total NUMERIC NOT NULL);
INSERT INTO customers VALUES (1,'Asha Rao','Rajahmundry'),(2,'Bilal Khan','Hyderabad'),(3,'Chitra Das','Vijayawada'),(4,'Dev Patel','Mumbai');
INSERT INTO orders VALUES (101,1,120),(102,1,80),(103,3,50),(104,9,40);
SELECT c.customer_name, COUNT(o.order_id) AS order_count FROM customers AS c LEFT JOIN orders AS o ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id GROUP BY c.customer_id, c.customer_name ORDER BY c.customer_id;
```
Expected result: Asha Rao | 2; Bilal Khan | 0; Chitra Das | 1; Dev Patel | 0

This example previews an outer join and aggregation: COUNT of a non-NULL order key gives zero for customers without orders.

## Query behaviour, NULLs and dialect notes
The examples target SQLite. A join predicate that evaluates to TRUE forms a matching pair; FALSE or UNKNOWN does not. Outer joins create NULL-extended columns for missing partners. Use a non-nullable key from the missing side when detecting unmatched rows. Do not assume RIGHT or FULL OUTER JOIN exists in every engine version: SQLite supports them from 3.39.0; older SQLite releases can express a RIGHT JOIN by reversing inputs and using LEFT JOIN. For older engines without FULL OUTER JOIN, a carefully constructed UNION ALL of a left join and right-only rows may be required. Always verify the target DBMS documentation.

## Common mistakes
- Joining on the wrong columns or omitting part of a composite relationship.
- Forgetting that one-to-many matches produce multiple output rows.
- Using SELECT * in a report with repeated column names.
- Filtering a nullable right-side column in WHERE and unintentionally removing unmatched rows from a LEFT JOIN.
- Counting rows with COUNT(*) when COUNT(right_table.primary_key) is needed to count matches.

## Best practices
Use explicit JOIN syntax, meaningful aliases, qualified column names and a deliberate ON condition. Test with matched, unmatched and duplicate-match data. Add ORDER BY when output order matters; SQL does not guarantee row order without it.

